  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 103 PM EDT Mon Oct 5 2026\n\nSurface high pressure moves through tonight and shifts off the east, \nTuesday. This opens the door for warm advection to be on our \ndoorstep. Warm advection often pairs with entering cloud cover, \nusually of the mid to upper cloud variety. This time, both the RAP \nand the NAM are picking up on 850 mb moisture that remains overhead \nfor a good portion of the area before some upper moisture pushes \ninto northwestern areas. If that 850 mb moisture can condense into \nclouds, that really becomes a problem for creating frost overnight \ntonight because it affects especially those areas that have a chance \nto dip down into the upper 30s. Chances are at about 15 to 25 \npercent of less than 38F across parts of St Joseph, Branch, and \nHillsdale counties of MI. If about 3 hours of such \ntemperatures occurs, frost becomes more possible, but today's \nmodels came in a little warmer making it less possible. Soil \nmoisture content is lower across the area that could receive the\nfrost and therefore, could enhance radiational cooling. If \nradiational cooling is able to take effect because cloud cover \nstays away long enough or altogether and winds slacken enough to\nallow decoupling, this frost still occur. It probably stays \nmore relegated to outlying areas though. At this point, given\nthe above combination of factors, have held off with any frost \nheadlines. With the warm advection that ensues Tuesday, temps \nare forecast to warm through Wednesday, when mid to upper 70s \nare possible.\n\nA cold front washes out Wednesday to Thursday and brings high \ntemps back to the upper 60s and low 70s for Thursday and Friday.\nThen, another trough enters the Western US and warm advection \nbrings us back securely into the 70s for Saturday with low 80s \npossible on Sunday. The warm front is producing rain in model \noutput for Friday night/Saturday morning, but it appears to be \ntoo dry in our area to see the rain. It's also interesting to \nsee that the system in the south that tried to get up to the \narea next Sunday to provide some rain is staying just south of \nthe area as the dry air hangs tough. As long as this lasts, it \ncould be until the following week before we see any appreciable \nrain.",
